# Argon Master Prompt — Blaize UI/UX Redesign and End-to-End Feature Integration

## Your role

Act as a principal product designer, senior React/TypeScript engineer, desktop-agent architect, and QA engineer. Improve the existing Blaize project in place. The goal is a clean, polished, low-clutter UI **and** a product where its distinctive features work together through real workflows.

This is not a request to remove complexity by deleting features, produce a visual-only mockup, or rebuild Blaize from scratch. First inspect the existing project and map the current implementation. Preserve working code, state, contracts, assets, tests, and important product behaviour. Refactor components only when there is a clear benefit. Implement the design and wiring, run the available checks, and report what genuinely works.

The project contains React/Vite UI code and feature modules such as `MorrowCompanion.tsx`, `MorrowLive2DRig.tsx`, `LivingDesktopStudioPanel.tsx`, `SoulMdEditorPanel.tsx`, `SkillsConnectorsSchedulerView.tsx`, `BlaizeBrowserView.tsx`, `VSCodeIdeView.tsx`, `PluginsHubView.tsx`, `ProjectsAndArtifactsView.tsx`, `ArtifactSplitDrawer.tsx`, `MissionControlView.tsx`, `L3xiFinanceView.tsx`, `TrustAndLedgerView.tsx`, and `EvalsAndSleepView.tsx`. Inspect their actual code and existing tests before deciding what is missing.

The current `package.json` uses React, Vite, TypeScript, Motion, and Express, but does not itself declare an Electron runtime. The build log also identifies some platform behaviours as simulated. **Do not represent a Vite/browser simulation as a real native desktop feature.** Preserve the current browser preview where necessary, clearly label simulation, and define a platform adapter for native desktop functions. If implementing the Electron runtime is possible in this environment, make a real main/preload/IPC layer and test it; if not, document the exact native gaps and keep the UI/API boundary ready without fabricating support.

Do not make live Meta API calls, consume paid credits, or require API keys for this pass. Keep model-provider integration contracts intact and use the existing labelled demo/mock provider for UI testing. Never make a mock response look like a verified live action.

