## 6. Mission engine and Mission Control

Make the existing mission system feel simple to use but complete under the surface.

- User asks for a goal; Core creates a plan whose number of steps depends on the actual task, uncertainty, dependencies, available tools and permissions. Never force a fixed step count.
- Provide objective, success criteria, current step, next action, task state, relevant tool activity, checkpoint/resume, retry history, cancellation and verification outcome.
- Compact progress display by default; detailed step/event timeline expands on demand.
- Support `CREATED`, `PLANNING`, `RUNNING`, `WAITING_FOR_USER`, `WAITING_FOR_EXTERNAL`, `COMPLETED`, `FAILED`, and `CANCELLED` with correct transitions.
- Stop when complete; wait when a user choice/dependency is needed; do not retry forever.
- Provide pause, resume, cancel and emergency stop with real handlers.
- Never show “completed” because the model merely said it was done. Run deterministic post-condition checks and use the verifier when required.
- Keep action history and evidence attached to the relevant mission.

