Before redesigning, create a feature wiring inventory with these columns:

`Feature | Current implementation | Real / local / simulated / missing | UI entry point | State/data source | Handler/service | Test | Work required`

Inspect the existing modules and identify buttons that only change decorative state, dummy data, missing persistence, unconnected views, or simulated native interfaces. Then wire the features to the correct shared state/service. Do not duplicate independent state stores for the same Morrow conversation, mission, active project, or plugin.

Use typed contracts and validate inputs at boundaries. Keep UI components thin; route meaningful work through the relevant package/service. Use existing architecture unless a concrete defect requires a refactor.

Important: the archive currently appears to be a Vite/React project rather than a complete Electron package. Maintain a working web preview, but define clear capability boundaries:

- `WebPreviewPlatformAdapter`: features that can genuinely run in the browser; simulated native capabilities must be labelled.
- `ElectronDesktopPlatformAdapter`: real global shortcut, transparent always-on-top window, OS app discovery, native file access, persistent Chromium sessions, native notifications and approved desktop automation when implemented.

Do not access OS APIs from renderer code. Use validated IPC and a narrow preload bridge. If the current environment cannot run Electron, implement/test renderer and service logic, add the adapter contract, and list exactly what still needs native runtime testing. Do not fake a native desktop feature as complete.

